  "title": "The Bitter Lesson of Tool Calling",
  "event_hint": "Systematic evaluation of programmatic vs JSON tool calling across 14 LLMs on BFCL v4 benchmark",
  "problem": "Lack of systematic evaluation comparing programmatic tool calling (tools as code) to native JSON tool calling across model generations under real-world task conditions",
  "mechanism": "Programmatic tool calling exposes tools as typed Python stubs that models invoke through executable code, with execution and results handled in a single agent turn via subprocess, replacing rigid JSON function-call objects",
  "evidence": [
    {
      "claim": "11 of 14 models match or exceed baseline accuracy under programmatic tool calling on BFCL v4 subset",
      "value": 11,
      "baseline": 14,
      "condition": "309-entry BFCL v4 subset, 8 task categories",
      "source_locator": "Abstract, Introduction, Section 4.1"
    },
    {
      "claim": "GPT-5.6 family achieves 10.6% absolute improvement over JSON tool calling baseline",
      "value": 10.6,
      "baseline": "JSON tool calling",
      "condition": "GPT-5.6-Sol (72.2% → 82.8%), GPT-5.6-Terra (73.5% → 84.1%)",
      "source_locator": "Abstract, Table 2"
    },
    {
      "claim": "Programmatic tool calling achieves 18.8% absolute accuracy gap over JSON tool calling at chain lengths ≥12",
      "value": 18.8,
      "baseline": "JSON tool calling",
      "condition": "Sequential chaining tasks, 52 entries, chain lengths 2-20",
      "source_locator": "Introduction, Section 3.4"
    },
    {
      "claim": "JSON tool calling drops tool calls above model-specific fan-out threshold (N=70-72 for Claude Sonnet 5); programmatic maintains 100% at N=100",
      "value": 100,
      "baseline": "JSON tool calling drops calls at N=70-72",
      "condition": "Claude Sonnet 5, parallel fan-out with probe entries at N∈{60,70,72,75,100}",
      "source_locator": "Introduction, Section 3.4"
    },
    {
      "claim": "Under context flooding, programmatic tool calling stable (+5.5%), JSON degrades 2.3%, filesystem-discovery degrades 32%",
      "value": 5.5,
      "baseline": "JSON tool calling -2.3%, filesystem-discovery -32%",
      "condition": "Context rot ablation, 31 entries per condition, 128 total schemas (flood vs filtered)",
      "source_locator": "Introduction, Section 3.4"
    }
  ],
  "evaluation_context": "309-entry BFCL v4 subset across 8 task categories (simple_python, multiple, parallel, parallel_multiple, live_simple, live_multiple, live_parallel, live_parallel_multiple). 14 models evaluated spanning Anthropic (5 models) and OpenAI (9 models) from November 2024 to July 2026. All experiments at temperature 0. Ablation studies: chaining (n=52, chain lengths 2-20), parallelism (n=32 enumeration entries, fan-out 7-48 plus probes), context rot (n=31 per condition). Deterministic scorer with normalized string comparison. 95% Wilson confidence intervals reported.",
  "limitations": "Results divide along model generation lines: all Anthropic models and three newest GPT generations match/exceed baseline, while three older GPT models (GPT-4o, GPT-4.1, GPT-5.4-mini) do not. Small ablation sample sizes (n=31-52) produce wide confidence intervals; only cross-model aggregate patterns treated as reliable. Evaluation limited to representative BFCL v4 subset, not full benchmark. No evidence presented on end-to-end task latency or token consumption—only accuracy metrics reported. Production deployment costs and error recovery not evaluated.",
  "project_relevance": "Directly relevant to Agent工具执行链优化. Programmatic tool calling addresses the topic's core question about whether tool calling parallelization introduces correctness risks by demonstrating it matches or exceeds JSON baseline in 13/14 models under fan-out conditions. However, the paper does not report Read/Grep/tool call counts, token consumption, or end-to-end latency—key metrics in the project context. The mechanism (single-turn code execution vs multi-turn JSON calls) may reduce redundant reads and context-building overhead in code agents, but this is not empirically measured in the visible evidence.",
  "primary_source_resolved": true,
  "quality_score": 75,
  "evidence_gaps": [
    {
      "question": "What are the token consumption and latency differences between programmatic and JSON tool calling paradigms?",
      "terms": ["token", "latency", "inference", "cost", "throughput", "turn", "agent loop"]
    },
    {
      "question": "What are the detailed accuracy results by task category and model generation?",
      "terms": ["simple_python", "multiple", "parallel", "live_simple", "live_multiple", "live_parallel", "category", "per-model", "breakdown"]
    },
    {
      "question": "What are the error rates and failure modes (syntax errors, runtime errors, hallucinated functions) for programmatic tool calling?",
      "terms": ["syntax error", "runtime error", "subprocess", "failure mode", "hallucinated", "stub"]
    }
  ]
}

